---
title: Text projection
---

## Overview {#sec-overview}

A paragraph that points at @fig-scree, so the projection must show the resolved
figure number rather than the raw `@`-reference.

::: {.callout-note}
## Heads up

Callouts carry a kind, and the text projection labels them by it.
:::

![A scree plot of the eigenvalues.](scree.png){#fig-scree}

The relation is exact:

$$E = mc^2$$

```{python}
x = 1
print(x)
```

## Structured blocks {#sec-structured}

The projection keeps these apart instead of fusing them:

- **name**: the column to reference.
- **Returns**: an `Expr` you can compare and combine.

{{< input name="lr" type="slider" min="0.01" max="0.35" step="0.01" value="0.12" label="step size" >}}

```{js}
//| input: lr
const lr = tali.value("lr");
const xs = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];
const data = xs.map((x) => ({ x, y: 6 - x * lr * 8 }));
return Plot.plot({
  height: 220,
  marks: [Plot.line(data, { x: "x", y: "y" }), Plot.dot(data, { x: "x", y: "y" })],
});
```

The landscape. Descent begins high on the steep left wall of the valley.

Which way is downhill. The negative gradient points almost straight across.
